WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.919
Economists tend to focus on things

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.029
that can be measured.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.569
Dignity is hard to measure.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.179
A sense of self is hard to measure.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.769
Belonging is hard to measure.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.449
A feeling of transcendence is hard

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.229
to measure.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.089
Mattering, that

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.279
you are important, that people

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.359
look to you.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.339
They're about the life well lived.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.959
And they're not about getting the

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:19.919
most out of your money.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.239
They're not about what the interest

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.649
rates are next week.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.509
And economists truthfully have

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:25.919
virtually nothing to say about these

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.609
things.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.619
Economist Russ Roberts is well known

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.479
for his extraordinary gift at

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.919
finding creative ways of

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.509
communicating the power of free

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.369
market capitalism to the general

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:35.999
public.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.009
Bread eaters will rejoice when

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.079
they see that every bakery

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.119
is filled with so much choice.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.559
He's the host of the wildly

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.539
successful podcast Econ Talk,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.249
which has been cranking out weekly

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.499
episodes since 2006.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.879
I hope our listeners find it

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:51.789
fascinating, too.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.339
If you do find this interesting,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.989
please send an email to me.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.009
Russell Roberts.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.569
Is the author of two novels.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.949
The Invisible Heart and Economic

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.839
Romance is one of those classic

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.149
boy meets girl stories.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.079
But in this case, the boy is an

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.969
economist. So it's kind of

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.859
a handicap that evokes all

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.509
the classic love stories of the past

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:10.529
involving economists.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.929
I can't think of any either.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.309
And along with filmmaker John

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.829
Coppola, he created the blockbuster

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.449
Keynes Hayek rap videos.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.039
We've been going back and forth for

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.149
a century.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.679
I want to steer markets.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.999
I want them set free.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.409
There's a boom and bust cycle.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.579
And good reason to fear it.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:27.399
I've made my case.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.769
Freddie H. Listen up.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.459
Can you hear it?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.019
More recently, his interest has

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.119
turned to the fundamental inadequacy

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.979
of his chosen discipline to

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.989
comprehend what matters most to

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.849
people. Robert's new book is called

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.739
Wild Problems, and it deals

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.809
with the decisions that define us.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.519
Whether to marry, whether to have

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.189
kids. What kind of work to pursue?

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.749
He says these are the sorts of

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.729
central questions that can't

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.199
be figured out with economic

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.659
modeling and cost-benefit analysis.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.559
Reason talked with

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:04.065
the 68-year-old Roberts about how he makes sense of a world that is richer than ever in material resources and yet suffers increasing numbers of deaths of despair. We discuss his own life from earning a Ph.D. in economics at the University of Chicago in the 1970s to starting econ talk to becoming president of a private liberal arts college in Israel, to the central role that religion plays in his life.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.239
Russ Roberts, thanks for talking to

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.529
Reason.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.819
Great to see you again, Nick.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:30.779
It's been a long time.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.159
It has been.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.259
But the new book is Wild

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.309
Problems.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.649
Give me the elevator pitch.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.329
So there are elevator pitches that

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.979
there are a number of aspects of our

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.439
lives, big decisions we face

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.359
that we'd like to have data

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.009
and evidence and make a rational,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.839
logical decision about them.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.549
These are decisions like whether

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:51.479
they have children, whether to

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.039
marry, who to marry, what career to

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.179
pursue.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.699
And there's not that many

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.519
pieces of evidence.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.159
And if I may in the case of Francis

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.079
Bacon, who comes up whether

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.849
to marry a child.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:03.850
Yeah.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.279
Am I wrong?

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.269
Yeah. I learned a little too much

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.129
about Francis Bacon in this book.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.759
This is a man who said, if you

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.679
marry, you became a hostage

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.879
to fortune.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.829
And by fortune, he did not mean

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:17.909
riches.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.219
He meant the randomness of life.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.319
Luck. Good luck.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.939
Bad luck. So he said, Why don't you

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.889
marry? You're a hostage to fortune.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.189
He married late in life and he

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:28.489
well.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.509
He was late in life.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.739
She was 45.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.569
She is 14 plus

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.037
or minus six months, right?

00:03:35.038 --> 00:03:36.059
But he saw her.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.529
But he saw her, he had his eye on

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.629
her when she was 11.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.009
They didn't have the best

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:41.969
relationship, which.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.039
Is the Enlightenment's version

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.599
of Georgy Girl or something?

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.609
It's just it's a very disturbing

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:48.688
scenario.

00:03:48.689 --> 00:03:49.409
It's a British film, right?

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.489
It is a British film.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.319
Yeah. With that Sr. James Mason.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.320
Yeah,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.729
well, I Francis Bacon is also

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.349
we're getting ahead of ourselves as

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.869
always, but he's one of Hayek's

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.309
enemies, so he's like an

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.169
epistemological enemy of Friedrich

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.149
Hayek. Who thought that Bacon

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.709
kind of ushered in all that was

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.299
wrong about enlightenment, thinking

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.889
and scientism, and whatnot?

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.079
But to come back while

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.959
problems are problems that

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.369
that resists measurement.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.518
Yes. And so talk about

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.529
and you do spend a lot of time early

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.479
on talking about like, okay, how,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.769
you know, how do you make a decision

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.779
whether or not to get married and

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.819
Charles Darwin even more than Bacon

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.709
is kind of your you know, one of the

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.629
one of the textbook cases you work

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:34.499
through.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.939
Yeah. Talk a little bit about that

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.559
and how that kind of explains the

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.389
larger set of issues that you're

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.309
trying to get at with Wild Problems.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.659
So I used Darwin as an example

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.769
because I think most people would

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.509
agree he is perhaps the greatest

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:46.739
scientist of all time.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.359
Okay, maybe second or third if

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.129
you're a skeptic about that, but

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.779
he's clearly a rational person.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.869
And I use a number of scientists

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.119
and analytical thinkers and

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.679
academics who struggle.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:00.849
With these kind of decisions that

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.709
we're talking about because they're

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.889
used to using analytical thinking

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.059
in the rest of their life.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.089
But when it comes to these kind of

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.189
decisions, they're kind of

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.029
unmoored at sea.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.889
And so Darwin tries to do what

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.269
most economists or other people

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.289
would say is rational.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:18.609
And you'll get this advice all the

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.559
time make a pro-con list,

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.729
make a list of the costs of the

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.559
benefits, and weigh them in

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.299
and see what's the right choice.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.139
And he does that.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:26.919
He makes a list.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.309
It's embarrassing.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.119
He compares horribly embarrassing.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.039
He says coming home to a wife

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.179
would be better than a dog, at

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.180
least.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.169
Kind of a low bar for

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:39.170
a spouse.

